  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Saturday...\n\nAn upper level low currently over western MT is bringing cool and wet conditions\nwith the help of remnant moisture from hurricane Rachel. Moderate forcing over \nsouth-central MT and easterly low level winds will allow for upslope snow for \neast facing mountains and foothills, especially for the Crazy mountains and \nsurrounding higher hills. Current warnings and advisories look good as they \nstand and no changes are anticipated at this time. Into the overnight hours \nslightly cooler air is expected to move into the low levels bringing snow and \na rain snow mix at times for more of south-central MT. Snow accumulations will \nbe difficult and less than a half an inch is still expected for the Billings \narea. As far as liquid precipitation goes, the best chances for an inch remain \nfor south-central MT at about 40-80%, with about a 20-40% chance for at least \na quarter inch across southeast MT through Monday morning. \n\nSubsidence looks to move across southern MT/northern WY Monday morning, allowing \nfor precipitation to exit the region by end of morning. This subsidence will \nalso bring breezy conditions to southern MT/northern WY with wind gusts 30-45 mph \nexpected with the strongest gusts expected in Sheridan Co, WY and southern Big \nHorn County. Higher heights will begin moving in Tuesday and Wednesday, but \ncool overnight temps in the 30s will remain in place through most of the work \nweek. Even though heights begin to build, the same low will slide across WY, \nbringing small to moderate precip chances to southern MT and northern WY Tuesday \nthrough Thursday morning. For the end of the week, ensembles and clusters \nshow high confidence for ridging, allowing for dry conditions and above \naverage temperatures in the upper 60s and 70s. TS",
